#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "Which of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/87979b85b4ea12aacf6bd158adc3fcb2.png)" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1$, we get $xy = -1$, which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0$, we get $xy = 0$, that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1$, we get $xy =1$, that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2$, we get $xy =-2$, that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5691898f126e352c214c8c48bb94c312.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0$, and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0$.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/54fea7979eb64dee5d535a276bd80c43.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/55af8d7d2226cd79132c79e19bbce747.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(e^{2x - 3y}) \\\\\n&= 2e^{2x - 3y} \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(e^{2x - 3y}) \\\\\n&=-3e^{2x - 3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 2 {e}^{2 x - 3 y} \\, \\mathbf{i} - 3 {e}^{2 x - 3 y} \\, \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "Which of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/ae5318226e1538c2b166e0591310b036.png)" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(2y^{2} - x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-2x \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(2y^{2} - x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = - 2 x \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 y \\, \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f$.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( - 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , - 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 2 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5cb2c61d730527746c2d42d5a8af642e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bdf2453-3eb2-5091-971a-54ba1490626b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}j + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177377} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6b264a-e2c4-5b19-8fd2-b8a71f9540ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{2y - z}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177378} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e2a659-2ae7-5c21-8389-0401730f02f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}j + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177379} {:answer/id #uuid "f37c10fc-d6f5-5508-8848-a5d7ed2cc3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177380} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdbad20-4755-595a-b905-15f95f29fe58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fdbad20-4755-595a-b905-15f95f29fe58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177381} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7593c80-6a81-56f5-a768-6ee89e694a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177376}] :question/id #uuid "68db203b-e688-5d3a-9ec7-9ce2f2bf5402" :question/math-academy-id "q-122404" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y, z) = x\\ln (z - 2y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1095d8-293f-5aa8-8c64-7e6dd979e26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177413} {:answer/id #uuid "ded0c347-d6b1-5426-a22f-1727a7ae3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177414} {:answer/id #uuid "0930e2cd-ff9c-5506-b300-1ec0f467cbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177415} {:answer/id #uuid "274c69bb-d582-5b25-ab97-68d93734d553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-5.png" :db/id #